{Transaction Information}
\label{dfn:InputInfo}
Transaction are a tuple providing:
\begin{description}
  \item[validity internal]
        execution time bounds. When missing, they are assumed to by minus
        infinity and plus infinity.
  \item[value] the amount of value in the transaction
  \item[fee] the fee of the transaction
  \item[validator] who is signing the transaction
  \item[transaction id]
  \end{description}

Tokens are structures containing a name, (polymorphic) data and id.
\label{dfn:Tokens.Elem.TokenD}


Tokens are records with:
\begin{description}
  \item[id] same as its reference
  \item[data] keeping internal data
  \item[name] the name of the token
\end{description}

\label{dfn:MUList.State.Elemt}


List nodes are records with:
\begin{description}
  \item[key] (optional) indicates the current node key,
  \item[link] (optional) indicates the next key in the list,
  \item[data] polymorphic element depending on the use of the list.
\end{description}

\label{dfn:MUList.State.Actions}


We have the following actions.
\begin{description}
  \item[Modify Data] modifies the data field of a node in the list
  \item[Unsafe Append] adds an element at the end of the list
  \item[Unsafe Prepend] adds an element at the beginning of the list
  \item[Remove] removes an element from the list
  \end{description}

\label{thm:MUList.Properties.Prepend_Props}



After prepending an element, we have a token showing its membership.

Furthermore, the root node is a valid anchor for the recently prepended node.

  \label{thm:MUList.Properties.Removes_members}



  After removing an element, the element must have had been a member of the
list.

  \label{thm:MUList.Properties.Removes_anch}



  If we can remove an element, then the anchor proposed is a valid anchor of the
element to be removed.

  \label{thm:MUList.Properties.Removes_update_anch_mem_and_key}



  After removing an element, its anchor links to the element the removed node
was linking to, and everything else remains the same.

  \label{thm:MUList.Properties.Removes_members_is_mem}



  If an element is a member of the resulting list after removing an element,
then it is a member of the original list.

  \label{thm:MUList.Properties.Removes_get_not_removed}



  If an element \(e\) is a member of a list after removing an element \(r\), then the
element \(e\) is not the same as the removed one, i.e. \(e \neq r\).

  \label{thm:MUList.Properties.ModifyData_find_token}



  If we can modify an element, the element is a member of the list before and
after modifying it.

\label{thm:MOList.Properties.non_membership_witness}



Given a token whose key is \(k\) and link \(nk\),
all keys \(q \in (k,nk)\) are not members of the list.

  \label{thm:MOList.Properties.insert_non_member}



  It is always possible to insert an element for which we have a witness stating
that there is not in the list.

  \label{thm:MOList.Properties.inserted_non_member}



  If we just inserted an element, then such element was not in the list before
inserting.

  \label{thm:MOList.Properties.insert_new_iff}



  After inserting an element, an element belongs to the resulting list if it is
either the just inserted node or another belonging to the previous list.

  \label{thm:MOList.Properties.insert_diff_same_old}



  After inserting, all members of the next list, but the just inserted one,
belongs to the list before inserting.

  \label{thm:MOList.Properties.OrdRemove_UordRemove}



  Ordered List action removes is implementing using Unorederd List action
remove.

  \label{thm:MOList.Properties.AfterRemove_TId_not_member}



  After removing an element from the list, it is not possible to use the token
to prove membership.

  \label{thm:MOList.Properties.OrdRemoves_members_back}



  After removing an element, if an element is a member of the new list, then it
belonged also to the list before removing an element.

{Parameters}
\label{dfn:MidgardParameters}


Record defining each parameter of a given Midgard instance.

\label{dfn:OperatorDirectory.State.OperatorDirectory}

We define operators directory as three lists: one unordered for registered
operators, and two ordered for active and retired operators.


  \label{dfn:OperatorDirectory.Relation}




  Defining transitions based on Midgards parameters, input information from the
	Cardano L1 transaction, and information provided by the user.


\label{thm:OperatorDirectory.Computational.ComputationalRelation}




There is a function realizing the computation of the Operators Directory
inductive relation.



  \label{thm:OperatorDirectory.Properties.Actions.activate_register_non_retired}
  If we can activate an operator then we know the operator is not a member of
the retired list.



  \label{thm:OperatorDirectory.Properties.Actions.retired_retired_step}

  Retiring an operator moves the operator to the retired list maintaining the
same information.




  \label{thm:deregister_register}
  Deregistering an operator removes the registered node, updates the anchor node
properly and keeps the rest of the nodes untouched.



  \label{thm:OperatorDirectory.Properties.Actions.removeduplicatereg_removes_dup_registered}


  Removing duplicated registered operators removes a node from the registered
operators queue.



\label{thm:OpsDir.OptimisticDetection.find_witness_valid}

The witness found generates a \emph{valid witness}.

It finds two different tokens with matching keys.



\label{thm:OpsDir.OptimisticDetection.find_witness_key}

The witness found identified a registered operator to penalized.

In other words, the operator to penalize is a member of the registered operator list.



  \label{thm:OpsDir.OptimisticDetection.find_witness_registered}

  The witness found has a valid anchor for the operator the
system is penalizing.



  \label{thm:OpsDir.OptimisticDetection.find_witness_dup_act}

  If the witness found is marked as registered and active duplication, then the witness has
two distinct tokens showing duplication of a registered operator and an active operator.



\label{dfn:Scheduler.State.SchedulerDatum}


The scheduler state is simply a record of values:
\begin{description}
    \item[operator\_id] indicating the current operator's id
    \item[shift\_start] indicating when the shift starts.
  \end{description}



\label{dfn:Scheduler.StateMachine.next}

State machine producing the next state triggered by two operations:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Advance
    \item Rewind
  \end{enumerate}




  \label{thm:Scheduler.Properties.ShiftStepShiftInterval}

  At each step, the new shift starts when the previous ended shifted by
\emph{shift\_duration}.



  \label{thm:Scheduler.Properties.ClaimSchedulerRewind}

  The last active operator rewinds the scheduler when there are no registered
operators waiting to be activated.

\label{dfn:StateQueue.State.Actions}

Operators can interact with the state queue as follows:
\begin{description}
  \item[CommitBlock] commiting a new block to the queue
  \item[MergeToConfirmed]  merging the first waiting block into the history
  \item[RemoveFraudulentBlockRetired] removing fraudulent blocks
  \item[RemoveFraudulentBlockActive] removing fraudulent blocks
\end{description}

  \label{thm:StateQueue.Props.block_merging_order}


  The block being merged into the history is the first node in the state queue
list.



  \label{dfn:FraudProofTokens.State.FraudProofTokens}
  The module state is just a collection of tokens indicating the hash of a
fraudulent block.



\label{dfn:FraudProofTokens.State.FraudProofTokens}
There is only one action minting fraudproof tokens.

The only condition is to provide a successfully finished computation thread.



